{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  // The shared markdown pipeline lives under app/ so the Worker can import it,
  // but the build scripts import it too, so this project must list it as well.
  // Named exactly, not globbed: app/lib/content also holds wasm.server.ts, which
  // is Worker-only and whose .wasm import does not resolve in this project.
  "include": [
    "vite.config.ts",
    // The Worker test layer's config. It runs in node like vite.config.ts does,
    // and it carries the alias and binding decisions the layer depends on, so a
    // typo in it should fail here rather than at the next test run.
    "vitest.config.ts",
    "scripts/**/*",
    // The shared security-headers package, which the header gates and their tests import.
    "packages/**/*",
    // The site origin. `ship.mjs` polls the deploy it just made, so the copy
    // that goes stale is the one that polls the wrong host and reports a
    // healthy site nobody is looking at. It imports the constant rather than
    // restating it, so this project has to reach the module.
    "app/lib/seo.ts",
    // The owner's identity, derived from the CV: `app/lib/seo.ts` and `app/lib/cv/entries.mjs` import both, so this
    // project must reach them. The generated one is written by `build:identity` (build:content and install).
    "app/lib/identity.mjs",
    "app/lib/identity.generated.mjs",
    // The terms path, which `app/lib/seo.ts` builds LICENSE_URL from and export-response.mjs puts in its Link header.
    "app/lib/license.mjs",
    // Listed because `app/lib/seo.ts` IMPORTS it, not because a script here
    // calls it, which is the same reason `app/lib/media/byte-size.mjs` is
    // listed below. `publicationsJsonLd` decodes character references so the
    // schema.org `headline` it emits is the string a human reads rather than
    // the escaped form the corpus stores, and this project must reach every
    // file a listed file reaches. Dependency-free, so it costs nothing.
    "app/lib/publications/entities.mjs",
    // Also imported by `app/lib/seo.ts`: `isSiteOwner` matches authors through
    // the exact alias table here, so this project must reach it too.
    "app/lib/publications/authors.mjs",
    // Also imported by `app/lib/seo.ts`: the list page types each paper with the same `schemaTypeFor` its
    // own page uses, so one `@id` is never two types. Imports only entities.mjs.
    "app/lib/publications/article-json-ld.mjs",
    // Every publication URL and file path, stated once. `check:machine-readable`
    // asserts that each hosted `pdfPath` is the path `paperPdfPath` derives, so
    // the gate has to reach the one function that owns the rule rather than
    // restate it. That binding is what stops `citation_pdf_url` pointing into a
    // directory Google Scholar will not accept.
    "app/lib/publications/paths.mjs",
    // The citation exports, and the three files they reach. `check:machine-readable`
    // generates BibTeX, RIS and CSL JSON twice and compares bytes, asserts one
    // entry per shown record, and asserts that organism names are
    // brace-protected THROUGH the matcher the writer uses rather than through a
    // substring test. Every one of those needs the real module, so this project
    // has to list it and everything it imports.
    // The Highwire tag builder. `check:machine-readable` exercises it over every
    // record and asserts the route calls it, because React Router's meta()
    // output is not in the markup a component render produces, so the tag set
    // cannot be checked against rendered HTML offline.
    "app/lib/publications/citation-tags.mjs",
    // The accession reader and the retraction notice. check:machine-readable
    // reconciles the corpus against what the data-availability statements name,
    // through the same function the page links from, and validates every
    // notice through the same one the page renders. Both are the shape this
    // project lists everything for: a gate must not check against a second
    // implementation of what it is checking.
    "app/lib/publications/accessions.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/update-notice.mjs",
    // The markdown twin builder and the cited-by reader it takes its count
    // from. compile.mjs writes each twin with them, for the build, the sync, the
    // gates and the Carrel adapter's save alike: one owner of what a twin says.
    "app/lib/publications/twin.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/cited-by.mjs",
    // How a paper's indexable text is assembled. build-content.mjs emits the
    // paper search records from it, on exactly the footing colophon-sections
    // and projects-page are listed on below: records.mjs is the one indexer and
    // must not learn a corpus's field names, so the assembly is a module and
    // both projects reach it.
    "app/lib/publications/search-inputs.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/exports.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/export-response.mjs",
    // The publication index's listing. check:machine-readable compares its
    // SHOWCASE_TYPES with export-response.mjs's by importing it, which a route
    // module (React, a loader) could not be.
    "app/lib/publications/listing.mjs",
    // Imported by exports.mjs for the brace-protection allowlist, and by the
    // gate through it. The page italicizes from the same list.
    "app/data/organisms.ts",
    "app/lib/content/pipeline.mjs",
    // Split out of pipeline.mjs, which imports and re-exports them, so this
    // project reaches them through it.
    "app/lib/content/content-error.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/corpus.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/directives.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/url-policy.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/slug.mjs",
    // Split out of pipeline.mjs so the editor can show a live reading time
    // without pulling shiki into a browser bundle. The pipeline imports it, so
    // this project has to reach it too.
    "app/lib/content/reading-time.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/chart.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/chart-types.mjs",
    // The operator's words for the five health checks, in one module rather
    // than retyped where they render.
    "app/lib/admin/check-copy.mjs",
    // The KaTeX options and the html-side `hasMath` reader. Three callers, which
    // is why it is its own module and why it is listed here: the pipeline passes
    // KATEX_OPTIONS to both the validator and rehype-katex, blog-view.ts asks
    // htmlHasMath on every post request, and check:content makes the two
    // derivations of that flag argue. Dependency-free by design, so listing it
    // costs this project nothing.
    "app/lib/content/math.mjs",
    // The feed item shapes. check:content asserts over the REAL corpus that
    // `mathToTex` leaves no KaTeX markup in a feed body, which needs the module
    // the feeds themselves call rather than a second transform that agrees with
    // it today. `.mjs` and dependency-free for the reason its own header gives.
    "app/lib/rss-feed.mjs",
    // How the social card's text is fitted and cut. `build:og` paints with it
    // and `ogImageKey` hashes through it, which is the whole reason it is a
    // module: the key must hash the string the template draws, not the field
    // it was drawn from. Both readers are in this project.
    "app/lib/content/og-card-text.mjs",
    // The one owner of "a timestamp as a date a person reads". The card draws
    // the publication date with it and the key hashes the STRING it returns, so
    // this project reaches it through pipeline.mjs and through build-og.mjs.
    "app/lib/long-date.mjs",
    // The two provenance hashes. The pipeline computes them into every record
    // and sync-content.mjs compares D1 against them at ship, so both projects
    // reach the module; one statement, per the module's own header.
    "app/lib/content/hashes.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/diagram.mjs",
    // Shared with the Worker's publication write: sync-content.mjs appends a long twin in these chunks.
    "app/lib/content/chunks.mjs",
    "app/lib/blog-listing.mjs",
    // The media library's view state. Its tests read PARAM_NAMES rather than
    // restating them, because a hand-written list drifting from the real one is
    // exactly what the two previous evaporation incidents were.
    // The server-side gate on irreversible actions. view.mjs re-exports
    // `confirmationSatisfied` from it, so this project has to reach it to
    // resolve that export at all.
    // The upload allowlist. check:headers asserts it holds no script-capable
    // type, so the origin invariant has one source rather than two agreeing lists.
    "app/lib/media/upload-contract.mjs",
    // Listed because upload-contract.mjs IMPORTS it, not because a script here
    // calls it: `validateUpload` composes the too-large sentence and the one
    // owner of "this byte count, as a size a person reads" is this module. A
    // project must list every file it reaches, so a transitive import of a
    // listed file lands here too. Dependency-free, so it costs nothing.
    "app/lib/media/byte-size.mjs",
    "app/lib/destructive.mjs",
    // The two webmention retention windows. The admin page labels its sweep
    // with them rather than with a copy of the sentence, on exactly the footing
    // CACHE_SENTENCE below is on: a paraphrase in the route that hard-coded 30
    // and 90 would be a second owner of a number the one-owner rule gives to
    // one module. Constants and one pure helper,
    // no Env and no DOM.
    "app/lib/webmention/retention.mjs",
    "app/lib/media/view.mjs",
    // The folder table view.mjs groups by. Same reason as view.mjs above:
    // the Node side reaches the module, so this project has to list it.
    "app/lib/media/folders.mjs",
    // The repository-reference scan. build:template-refs walks the tree and
    // check:content reconciles the artifact, so both reach this module, and the
    // Worker imports the artifact it produces. One definition of "does source
    // reference this asset", for the same reason classify.mjs is listed: a gate
    // and a writer disagreeing about it is the drift the gate exists to catch.
    "app/lib/media/template-refs.mjs",
    // The three-state usage model, the per-row flags and the copy snippets. The
    // route renders from it.
    "app/lib/media/usage.mjs",
    "app/lib/search/records.mjs",
    // The Research and Teaching page list, which scripts/build-content.mjs validates against.
    "app/lib/content-pages.mjs",
    // How a dictionary entry reads in a twin, a search record and the DefinedTerm, which content-pages.mjs leads with.
    "app/lib/dictionary-entries.mjs",
    // The CV: its source types, the validator, the compile door, the resolved entries and the markdown.
    // build:content, sync:content and build:cv-pdf run the SAME modules the route renders and the save
    // compiles with, which is what stops them saying different things (docs/CV.md). No DOM and no Worker types.
    "app/lib/cv/types.ts",
    "app/lib/cv/parse.mjs",
    "app/lib/cv/validate.mjs",
    "app/lib/cv/compile.mjs",
    "app/lib/cv/entries.mjs",
    "app/lib/cv/view.mjs",
    "app/lib/cv/markdown.mjs",
    // The print document and the fingerprint, which the Worker and scripts/build-cv-pdf.mjs both draw from.
    "app/lib/cv/pdf-html.mjs",
    // The no-wide-dash rule, which the pipeline re-exports and the CV validator imports without the renderer.
    "app/lib/content/wide-dash.mjs",
    // The Ask item key mapping. check:machine-readable round-trips every paper's
    // page URL through it, because the key is the citation: AI Search carries
    // nothing else about where a chunk came from, so a key that does not map
    // back to the page cites a URL that 404s, and only a reader who clicked
    // would ever find out. The uploader and the client chunk both import it,
    // which is why there is one module to reach.
    "app/lib/search/ask-keys.mjs",
    // The colophon's section descriptor. build-content.mjs reads it to emit the
    // page's search records, and app/routes/colophon.tsx renders from the same
    // list, which is what stops an anchor existing in two places.
    "app/lib/colophon-sections.mjs",
    // The roster's metric derivations. check:features computes them through
    // metricValue, and the public Software pages are markdown, not this module.
    "app/lib/projects-page.mjs",
    // The theme resolver. workers/app.ts builds its cache key with it and
    // root.tsx writes data-theme from it, and test/theme.test.mjs reaches the
    // same module rather than restating its rules. No DOM: it takes a Request,
    // which the Node lib provides.
    "app/lib/theme.ts",
    "app/lib/search/query.mjs",
    // The color maths. check:contrast computes with it, on the same principle as
    // query.mjs: there is one rule for a ratio, not one per reader.
    "app/lib/contrast.mjs",
    // What an asset IS. The Worker imports it and build:assets imports it, on
    // the same principle as query.mjs: there must never be a second answer to
    // "what kind of thing is this file".
    "app/lib/media/classify.mjs",
    // The transform ladders. The pipeline writes srcset from them and the Worker
    // serves from them, so both projects must reach the one definition: a width
    // the pipeline advertises but the route refuses is a 400 mid-article.
    "app/lib/media/widths.mjs",
    // The WebP quality every Images-binding call emits at. build:assets encodes
    // its placeholders with the SAME constant the Worker transforms with, which
    // is the whole reason it is a module rather than a literal in a route: it
    // had two call sites before this project ever reached it, and they
    // disagreed for months.
    "app/lib/media/encoding.mjs",
    // Where the asset manifest lives, POSIX and repo-relative. build:assets
    // writes it, check:content reconciles it and the Node resolver reads it;
    // the Worker asks GitHub for the same string. One statement, because a
    // path.join spelling would 404 on the contents API.
    "app/lib/media/manifest.mjs",
    // The operator publish policy: the Worker imports it and check:policy
    // imports it, on the same principle as query.mjs.
    "app/lib/editor/publish-policy.mjs",
    // The artifact read limits. github.server.ts enforces them on the wire and
    // check:content refuses at half the ceiling, so both projects must reach
    // the one statement of the number.
    "app/lib/editor/artifact-limits.mjs",
    // The editor's transition table. Same principle: the Worker imports it and
    // Node tooling imports it, so both projects must reach it.
    "app/lib/editor/publish-transition.mjs",
    // The Content Security Policy. The Worker builds every response header
    // from it and check:headers CALLS it once per branch, on the same
    // principle as publish-policy.mjs: the gate must not assert against a
    // second copy of the policy it is checking. It is under workers/ rather
    // than app/ because it is response policy, not routing.
    "workers/csp.mjs",
    // Which content types are served WITHOUT a policy. Same principle and the
    // same directory as csp.mjs: the Worker decides with it and check:headers
    // CALLS it against the content-type each feed route declares, rather than
    // reading the list's spelling out of app.ts. It is a separate module from
    // csp.mjs because the exemption is a property of the RESPONSE TYPE and not
    // of the policy, and because the drift it exists to catch is between the
    // list and the routes.
    "workers/feed-types.mjs",
    // Listed because csp.mjs IMPORTS it: the policy's script hash is computed
    // from the loader text this module holds, which is what keeps the two from
    // drifting. Dependency-free.
    "app/lib/enhance-loader.mjs",
    // Imported by enhance-loader.mjs for the URL prefix. Dependency-free.
    "app/lib/enhance-url.mjs",
    // Listed because csp.mjs IMPORTS it too: the speculation rules' hash is
    // computed from the builder SiteSpeculation renders with. Dependency-free.
    "app/lib/speculation.mjs",
    // Imported by speculation.mjs, which escapes its rules with it.
    "app/lib/json-ld.mjs",
    // Listed because csp.mjs IMPORTS it: `media-src` is written from the same
    // audio host list the podcast feed parser accepts episodes against, so the
    // policy cannot refuse audio the home page renders. Dependency-free.
    "app/lib/podcast/feed.mjs",
    // The ONE JavaScript owner of "is this post publicly visible". build:og
    // decides which posts get a social card with it and the Worker reads it
    // too, so both reach the same predicate. A second
    // copy of this rule is what put five drafts into Ask in July, and a card
    // for an unpublished post is the same leak by a different road.
    "app/lib/search/visibility.mjs",
    // The ratified secret list. check:secrets imports its expectation from it
    // and the Worker audits against it, so this project has to reach it.
    "app/lib/secrets.mjs",
    // The health snapshot's poll interval and staleness rule. The home tile
    // decides with it and check:browser asserts against it, so both projects
    // reach the one statement rather than the gate carrying a third copy of a
    // number that already has two owners. Named exactly, not globbed:
    // app/lib/health also holds checks.server.ts and snapshot.server.ts, which
    // are Worker-only and take an Env this project cannot resolve.
    "app/lib/health/snapshot.mjs",
    // Whether a failing health run may repair itself, and what a watchdog
    // firing DOES about it. Two callers reach this one module and that is why
    // it is here: the watchdog Worker (tsconfig.cloudflare.json) and test/. A second copy
    // of REPAIRABLE would be two answers to "may this fire an unattended write
    // against production", which is the one table where rule 17 is expensive.
    "app/lib/health/repair.mjs",
    // The Ask settle loop, which repair.mjs imports and ship's two Ask steps call: one cadence and
    // one window for the watchdog and the deploy.
    "app/lib/health/ask-converge.mjs",
    // The renamed-post redirect predicate. The gateway resolves every request
    // through it and check:urls asserts the map against the corpus with it, so
    // both projects must reach the one predicate. That is the whole reason it
    // takes the map as an argument rather than importing the JSON: a second
    // copy of "which slug does /blog/x mean now" would be free to disagree with
    // the Worker, and the disagreement would look like a 404 to a reader
    // following a published URL. Its one import is the redirect status rule below.
    "app/lib/slug-redirect.mjs",
    // The moved-sections rule, which the moved-paths browser case reads rather than restating.
    "app/lib/path-moves.mjs",
    // Imported by modules listed above, so this project must reach them too:
    // the status rule (slug-redirect), the thrown-value reader (pipeline,
    // upload-contract), the byte and hash encodings (classify, hashes, diagram,
    // pipeline) and the hast builders (chart, diagram). Each is dependency-free.
    "app/lib/https-redirect.mjs",
    "app/lib/error-message.mjs",
    "app/lib/bytes.mjs",
    "app/lib/content/hast.mjs",
    // check:links resolves every internal link through the gateway's own redirect rules and the
    // site's own path builders, and reads the header menus as they render.
    "app/lib/wordpress-redirects.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/pdf-redirect.mjs",
    "app/lib/tag-path.mjs",
    "app/lib/series-path.mjs",
    "app/lib/nav.ts",
    // The procedure system's compile path. check:protocols, build:content and sync:content compile every
    // procedure with the SAME modules the operator API's save_procedure runs, so a file CI passes is the
    // file the save tool accepts. No Env and no DOM.
    "app/lib/procedures/marks.mjs",
    "app/lib/procedures/parse.mjs",
    "app/lib/procedures/validate.mjs",
    "app/lib/procedures/taxonomy.mjs",
    "app/lib/procedures/proof.mjs",
    "app/lib/procedures/render.mjs",
    "app/lib/procedures/compile.mjs",
    "app/lib/procedures/cite.mjs",
    // The publication file: parsed, judged and compiled by one door that the gates, the build, the sync and the
    // Carrel adapter all call (docs/PUBLICATIONS.md). The content-id rule is shared with the adapter's registry.
    "app/lib/publications/parse.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/validate.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/record.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/serialize.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/topics.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/hosting.mjs",
    "app/lib/publications/types.ts",
    "app/lib/publications/compile.mjs",
    "app/lib/carrel/content-id.mjs",
    // The page system's compile path. build:content and sync:content compile every page with the SAME
    // modules the page save runs, so a file CI passes is the file the save accepts. invariants.mjs holds
    // the rules the CI tests used to read the files for, so it reaches the phage record and the calculators.
    "app/lib/pages/compile.mjs",
    "app/lib/pages/invariants.mjs",
    "app/lib/pages/links.mjs",
    // The llms.txt rules, shared by check:machine-readable and the llms.txt save (docs/LLMS.md).
    "app/lib/llms/validate.mjs",
    // The dictionary entries that open named Software pages: parsed and judged by one door the build, the sync and
    // the Carrel adapter all call (docs/DICTIONARY.md). The page compile leads a twin with the entry.
    "app/lib/dictionary/parse.mjs",
    "app/lib/dictionary/compile.mjs",
    "app/lib/phage-table.mjs",
    "app/lib/phage-tools.mjs",
    // The roster's compile path: build:content and sync:content compile every cohort file with the SAME module
    // the Carrel adapter's roster save runs (docs/ROSTER.md). scripts/lib/roster.mjs is included by the scripts project.
    "app/lib/roster/compile.mjs",
    // The phage table's compile path, the same on both sides (docs/PHAGES.md): the page compile draws the table from
    // it, so build:content and sync:content compile every phage file with the module the phage save runs.
    "app/lib/phages/compile.mjs",
    // The registry's compile path, the same on both sides (docs/REGISTRY.md): build:content and sync:content compile
    // every registry file with the module the registry save runs, and it reads its kinds from kinds.mjs.
    "app/lib/registry/compile.mjs",
    "app/lib/registry/kinds.mjs",
    // The primer kind and the Tm it computes, which kinds.mjs imports.
    "app/lib/registry/primer.mjs",
    "app/lib/registry/strain.mjs",
    "app/lib/registry/tm.mjs",
    // How the registry reads on the site: scripts/check-links.mjs names each page from it, and it reads the primer arithmetic.
    "app/lib/registry/catalog.mjs",
    "app/lib/primers.mjs",
    // Where a primer binds a reference and the product a pair makes (docs/REGISTRY.md), with the references it reads.
    "app/lib/registry/align.mjs",
    "app/lib/registry/reference.mjs",
    "app/lib/registry/references.generated.mjs",
    // What the site's search finds for an item, which build:content writes into the search artifact.
    "app/lib/registry/search-inputs.mjs"
  ],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "strict": true,
    "types": ["node"],
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ES2022",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler"
  }
}
